(Background: Following successful adaptation of Pal-Cycles across seven European countries,) Tj
T*
(this analysis presents clinical and implementation outcomes during stepped-wedge) Tj
T*
(implementation. Objective: To evaluate preliminary clinical effectiveness and) Tj
T*
(implementation metrics following adapted intervention deployment. Methods: Twelve-month) Tj
T*
(outcome data were collected from 180 advanced cancer patients and their providers across) Tj
T*
(all seven countries during stepped-wedge phase. Patient-reported measures included symptom) Tj
T*
(burden, care satisfaction, and quality of life. Provider-level metrics assessed care) Tj
T*
(coordination perception and workflow integration. Implementation fidelity was monitored) Tj
T*
(through audits of care documentation and intervention protocol adherence. Results:) Tj
T*
(Patient-reported care coordination improved by 3.2 points on an 11-point scale \(95% CI) Tj
T*
(1.8-4.6\). Symptom burden decreased by a mean 2.1 points on the Edmonton Symptom Assessment) Tj
T*
(System \(P = 0.042\). Provider satisfaction with care planning processes increased across) Tj
T*
(countries \(mean 6.8/10 baseline to 8.1/10 post-implementation\). Implementation fidelity) Tj
T*
(averaged 81% across sites \(range 74-91%\). No serious intervention-related adverse events) Tj
T*
(were identified. Conclusion: Adapted Pal-Cycles implementation demonstrated feasibility,) Tj
T*
(positive preliminary clinical outcomes, and moderate-to-high fidelity across diverse) Tj
